  "query_preview": "..\nTuesday and Wednesday: A broad upper-level trough crawling\ntoward the West Coast will direct shortwaves into British \nColumbia and the Inland Northwest, bringing periodic bands of \nhigh clouds but no precipitation. The arrival of a weak cold\nfront Tuesday afternoon will drop temperatures by 5-10 degrees\nand bring breezy westerly winds with sustained speeds of 10-15\nmph and gusts up to 25 mph. Temperatures cool by an additional\n2-5 degrees on Wednesday with afternoon highs in the low to mid\n70s for most locations.\n\nThursday through Sunday: A shortwave dropping down from the \nGulf of Alaska into the offshore trough on Thursday will dig \ninto the base of the trough, stretching it meridionally and \ntemporarily bringing higher heights back over the eastern third \nof WA and the ID panhandle. These higher heights will briefly \nwarm temperatures by 3-5 degrees on Thursday before the cooling\ntrend resumes Friday and Saturday. Further west over Central and\nWestern WA, temperatures will remain largely unchanged from\nWednesday into Thursday.\n\nThe offshore trough tracks inland Thursday night into Friday, \nsending a more robust cold front through the region. With the\ncold front passage will come gusty winds, noticeably cooler \ntemperatures, rain, and potential for high mountain snow. The \nlatest probabilities from the NBM show a 70-90% chance for wind\ngusts of at least 20 mph, a 30-70% chance for 30 mph, and \n15-30% chance for 40 mph. These probabilities have trended up\nfrom previous NBM runs.\n\nThe system will also deliver cooler temperatures with highs \ncooling from the 70s Wednesday and Thursday into the 60s Friday\nthrough the weekend. Overnight temperatures will decrease as\nwell with widespread lows in the mid 30s to low 40s expected\nstarting Friday night. Some mountain valleys may be flirting \nwith freezing overnight temperatures heading into the weekend. \n\nAs for precipitation, ensembles are indicating a 50-70% chance \nfor at least 0.10\" along the Cascade Crest and mountains of NE \nWA and N ID. The potential outcomes for these areas are as high \nas 0.50 inches or more. This could equate to several inches of \nsnow for some of the highest peaks. Confidence is much lower for\nrainfall amounts outside of these areas. Latest guidance has \ncontinued to increase the chances for 0.10\" of rain for lower\nelevations. Locations including Lewiston, Pullman, Spokane, \nKellogg, Colville, and Omak now have a 40-60% chance of\nreceiving at least 0.10\" of rain by Saturday morning. For\nlocations in the deeper Columbia Basin including Ephrata and\nMoses Lake, chances are between 30-40%. There is a slight\nchance for isolated thunderstorms over the northern mountains \nFriday with the frontal passage, but this comes with very low \nconfidence.\n\nSnow levels in the Cascades will drop near 6500 feet Friday and\nwill further decrease to around 5000 feet Friday night into \nSaturday morning. Those venturing into the high country should \nbe prepared for cooler temperatures and wet conditions. At this \ntime, snow accumulations look light, but a few of the highest \npeaks near the immediate Cascade crest could pick up several \ninches. /Fewkes",
